1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,360
This video recording focuses on two-level paging scheme, right?

2
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:22,560
And to understand why a multi-level paging scheme is necessary, let's look at an example.

3
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:36,240
So in this example, we assume a computer system with a 32-bit logical address and the page size is 4K byte.

4
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:39,680
So 4K is 2.12, right?

5
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:49,000
So given these assumptions, right, then we know, so there are 32 bits in the logical address, right?

6
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,560
Then they divide into two parts, so page number and offset.

7
00:00:53,560 --> 00:01:01,240
So page size is 2.12, so the lower 12 bits are for the offset and the remaining bits.

8
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:05,080
So that will be for the page number, right?

9
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:16,040
So the 32 bits in the logical address and the logical address space, that will be 2.32 bytes.

10
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:23,240
Page size is 2.12, so 2.32 divided by 2.12, so that's equal to 2.20.

11
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:29,320
So that basically means we have 2.20 pages in the logical address space.

12
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:37,800
As I already mentioned in the previous lecture, so there is one entry in the page table per page.

13
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:49,400
So if you have 2.20 pages in the logical address space, then correspondingly, then we will have 2.20 entries in the page table.

14
00:01:50,920 --> 00:02:04,040
So page table entry, so contains information such as the frame number where the corresponding page is in the physical memory, and also some other information which I will cover later on, right?

15
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:11,000
So each entry will occupy certain amount of space, right?

16
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,720
So in this case, let's assume each entry occupies 4 bytes.

17
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:30,920
So we have this amount of entries and each entry occupies 4 bytes, then in total, then the page table will require 4 times 2.20 bytes space, right?

18
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:33,640
So that's 4 megabytes.

19
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:37,400
So recall, page table needs to be in the memory, right?

20
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:47,640
Then to find a hole that large enough in the memory to accommodate the page table will become an issue, right?

21
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:54,200
So in this case, so we need to find a contiguous memory space, right?

22
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:57,480
So that's 4 megabytes, right?

23
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:03,060
To accommodate, to accommodate, to assign, to allocate to the page table.

24
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:06,000
So this will become a huge issue, right?

25
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:06,520
Big issue.

26
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:09,000
Now what we can do?

27
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:17,740
So what we can do is we can page this page, big page table into pages, right?

28
00:03:17,740 --> 00:03:24,800
Then load the pages of this big page table into available frames.

29
00:03:25,260 --> 00:03:30,460
So that means we don't need to assign contiguous memory space, right?

30
00:03:30,940 --> 00:03:34,220
In for the page table, right?

31
00:03:34,260 --> 00:03:39,680
So we load the pages of the page table into available frames, right?

32
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:46,580
So that's basically the motivation, right, behind multi-level paging, right?

33
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:48,680
So in this particular case, two-level paging.

34
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:53,800
Now let's look at how exactly this works, right?

35
00:03:54,460 --> 00:03:56,280
If you look at the example here, right?

36
00:03:56,380 --> 00:04:02,400
So in this example, right, so logic address space is 2.32 bytes, right?

37
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:11,940
So it's the two bits in the logical address, and each page occupies 4 times 2.10 bytes.

38
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:14,680
So that's 2.12 bytes.

39
00:04:15,580 --> 00:04:20,980
So this logical address space, then we'll be divided into 2.20 pages, right?

40
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:25,420
So 2.32 divided by 2.12, so that's equal to 2.20.

41
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:31,940
So correspondingly, then we have 2.20 entries, right, in the page table.

42
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:36,100
So this is a page table, this is a logical address space, page table.

43
00:04:36,780 --> 00:04:40,820
We have 2.20 entries in this page table.

44
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:50,700
If each entry occupies 4 bytes, then the size of this page table is 4 times 2.20 bytes, right?

45
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:56,640
So this page table is too big, then we divide this page table into pages,

46
00:04:56,640 --> 00:05:04,680
and load the page table into available memory frames, right?

47
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:09,500
But we need to remember where they are in the memory, right?

48
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:17,980
To remember where they are in the memory, in this case, we need another level of page table, right?

49
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:26,720
So this big page table, the size is 4 times 2.20 bytes, right?

50
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:29,560
Page size is 4 times 2.10 bytes.

51
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:35,920
So this big page table will be divided into 2.10 pages.

52
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:43,880
So correspondingly, in the next level of page table, then there will be 2.20 entries, right?

53
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:50,940
Now to do address translation, right, as we already covered, right?

54
00:05:50,940 --> 00:06:00,340
Then to locate, for example, this particular page of the logic address space in the memory,

55
00:06:00,980 --> 00:06:07,600
then we need a specific entry in the page table, right?

56
00:06:07,660 --> 00:06:14,200
The entry in the page table contains the frame where the page is located in the memory, right?

57
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:22,580
But to locate the page that contains this page entry in the big page table,

58
00:06:22,860 --> 00:06:31,000
where it is in the physical memory, then we need an entry in the second level of page table, right?

59
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:39,480
So the entry can be contains the frame where the page of the big page table is in the physical memory, right?

60
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:52,100
So for the big page table, we refer this as the first level page table or inner page table.

61
00:06:52,860 --> 00:07:06,660
And for the second level of page table, so this is also referred to as the outer page table, right?

62
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:14,820
So that's the arrangement for the multi-level paging.

63
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:19,520
So basic idea, divide the big page table into pages, right?

64
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:27,040
And load the pages of this big page table into available frames, right?

65
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:32,940
Now, as I just illustrated, right?

66
00:07:33,020 --> 00:07:37,800
To do address translation, then we need an entry in the first level of page table.

67
00:07:38,320 --> 00:07:43,100
To locate the page that contains this entry where it is in the physical memory,

68
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:46,180
then we need an entry in the second level of page table, right?

69
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:49,620
So the entry index, right?

70
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:54,880
So this is called P1 and this is called P2, right?

71
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:58,200
So we need this two index.

72
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:01,800
Now let's look at, right?

73
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:07,360
So given the logical address, right?

74
00:08:07,420 --> 00:08:10,100
So that's 32 bits in total, right?

75
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:13,600
Then if you're using this two-level paging,

76
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:19,240
then we need to divide the page number portion into two parts, right?

77
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:23,020
The lower 12 bits, so that's for the offset, right?

78
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:27,200
Because the page size is 2.12 bytes, right?

79
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:30,520
So we need the 12 bits for the offset.

80
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:39,140
Then the P1 is indexed into the second level of page table or outer level of page table.

81
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:44,660
So the number of bits is indexed into the inner level of page table.

82
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:51,960
Now let's try to figure out how many bits will be required for P1 and how many bits will be required for P2.

83
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:58,160
So the number of bits required for P1, if you look at the figure here,

84
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:07,760
that will determine by the number of entries, right, in this outer level or second level of page table, right?

85
00:09:07,760 --> 00:09:13,140
So in this case, we already figured out, so there will be two parts of 10 entries, right?

86
00:09:13,220 --> 00:09:15,700
So we need 10 bits for P1.

87
00:09:16,680 --> 00:09:20,660
So number of bits required for P2, right?

88
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:28,640
So that will determine by how many entries in a page of the first level of page table or the inner level of page table.

89
00:09:29,220 --> 00:09:32,320
So how many entries in a page, right?

90
00:09:32,740 --> 00:09:34,380
So we know the page size.

91
00:09:34,380 --> 00:09:38,540
So page size is 4 times 2 part of 10, without page size.

92
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:41,640
We also know the entry size, which is 4.

93
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:45,620
Then how many entries contain in a page?

94
00:09:46,280 --> 00:09:51,160
So that's basically is the page size divided by entry size, right?

95
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:52,460
So that's 2 part of 10.

96
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:54,840
So there are 2 part of 10 entries here.

97
00:09:55,040 --> 00:09:58,440
So that means for P2, we also need 10 bits.

98
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:06,480
So P1 is used as an index into the outer or second level of page table, right?

99
00:10:06,540 --> 00:10:14,220
In this case, right, so determined by the number of entries in the outer or second level of page table.

100
00:10:14,220 --> 00:10:22,180
So P2 is used as index into a page of the inner or first level of page table, right?

101
00:10:22,220 --> 00:10:31,180
So the number of entries is determined by how many entries in a page in the inner or first level of page table, right?

102
00:10:31,180 --> 00:10:40,180
Now let's look at finally the address translation by using this double level of page table, right?

103
00:10:40,180 --> 00:10:50,100
So given a logical address is divided into three parts, P1, P2, and D, right?

104
00:10:50,560 --> 00:11:02,600
So for the outer level of page table, we can locate where it is in the physical memory by using this page table base register, right?

105
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:13,600
So this is this register value pointing to the starting location of where the outer page table is in the physical memory.

106
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:20,920
Then P1 is used as index into this outer level of page table to locate entry.

107
00:11:21,820 --> 00:11:23,880
So this entry contains a frame number.

108
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:32,080
This frame number indicating where the required page of the first level of page table is.

109
00:11:32,600 --> 00:11:34,420
in the physical memory.

110
00:11:34,780 --> 00:11:40,720
So this is a page of the first level of page table or inner level of page table, right?

111
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:46,860
Then we know by using this frame number that we know where it is in the physical memory.

112
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:57,740
And the P2 is used as index to locate entry in this page of the first level of page table, right?

113
00:11:57,740 --> 00:12:01,140
And from there, we obtain the frame number.

114
00:12:01,880 --> 00:12:07,740
And together with the offset, then we form the physical address.

115
00:12:08,300 --> 00:12:08,500
Right.

116
00:12:08,960 --> 00:12:14,580
So what I explained here basically is how two-level paging works.

117
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:18,880
The same concept can also apply to three-level paging.

118
00:12:18,880 --> 00:12:23,000
So that means you page this page table again, right?

119
00:12:23,560 --> 00:12:26,840
And to generate another level of page table, right?

120
00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:31,040
Or even the four-level paging and so on.

121
00:12:32,460 --> 00:12:37,060
I understand this is a difficult topic, right?

122
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:38,220
To follow, right?

123
00:12:38,220 --> 00:12:41,520
And to help you to understand this better.

124
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:45,660
So in the supplement material folder,

125
00:12:46,280 --> 00:12:54,060
so there is a concrete example to illustrate how two-level paging actually works.

